1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:06,510
The brush clears the cut face of a wall — earth, brick, and the stumps of a timber palisade.

2
00:00:06,510 --> 00:00:08,680
Walls like this are expensive.

3
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:15,756
Somebody had a great deal of money, and a reason to be afraid.

4
00:00:15,756 --> 00:00:20,063
This episode is about how one state out of sixteen ended up with both.

5
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:30,826
Buddhist and Jain texts, which were written close to these events and name the kings, and excavation at the capitals.

6
00:00:30,826 --> 00:00:37,920
The dynastic details differ between sources, so dates here are approximate and the sequence matters more than the years.

7
00:00:41,867 --> 00:00:47,078
Start with why Magadha and not somewhere else, because this is really a geography answer.

8
00:00:47,078 --> 00:00:52,406
It sat where several rivers meet, so it controlled river trade and had water on its flanks.

9
00:00:52,406 --> 00:00:57,628
Its first capital was ringed by hills, which is a fortress you do not have to build.

10
00:01:01,900 --> 00:01:04,598
It had iron ore nearby, for weapons and tools.

11
00:01:04,598 --> 00:01:11,255
It had forests, for timber and for elephants — and war elephants were an advantage no neighbour could easily match.

12
00:01:11,255 --> 00:01:17,065
And it had the most fertile stretch of the Ganga plain, which means surplus, which means taxes.

13
00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:24,056
Then the rulers.

14
00:01:24,056 --> 00:01:26,368
The Haryanka dynasty comes first.

15
00:01:26,368 --> 00:01:30,885
Bimbisara expands by marriage alliances and by annexing Anga.

16
00:01:44,733 --> 00:01:50,150
His son Ajatashatru takes a harder line: he fights Kosala and breaks the Vajji confederacy,

17
00:01:50,150 --> 00:01:54,448
which takes years because a confederacy is hard to defeat in one battle.

18
00:01:54,448 --> 00:02:00,223
Udayin, who follows, is credited with founding a fort at Pataliputra, where the rivers meet.

19
00:02:02,900 --> 00:02:09,715
The Shishunagas follow, and are remembered chiefly for destroying the power of Avanti — removing Magadha's last serious rival.

20
00:02:09,715 --> 00:02:13,343
Then the Nandas, who are the ones the Greek accounts noticed.

21
00:02:17,567 --> 00:02:24,404
They built an enormous army and a famously large treasury, and they were widely disliked for the taxation that paid for both.

22
00:02:24,404 --> 00:02:30,021
When Alexander's soldiers heard what waited for them beyond the Beas, they refused to go further.

23
00:02:35,967 --> 00:02:39,172
Watch the capital move, because it tells the story.

24
00:02:39,172 --> 00:02:45,574
Rajagriha is defensive: a city inside a ring of hills, right for a state that is still vulnerable.

25
00:02:50,467 --> 00:02:57,348
Pataliputra is offensive and commercial: set where the rivers meet, it controls traffic and projects power downstream and up.

26
00:02:57,348 --> 00:03:04,748
A state that moves its capital from the hills to the river has stopped worrying about surviving and started thinking about reach.

27
00:03:12,333 --> 00:03:14,102
Pataliputra is Patna.

28
00:03:14,102 --> 00:03:16,091
Rajagriha is Rajgir.

29
00:03:18,867 --> 00:03:22,163
Both are in Bihar, and both are places you can still visit.

30
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:28,531
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

31
00:03:28,531 --> 00:03:30,454
Four reasons Magadha won.

32
00:03:30,454 --> 00:03:36,077
Iron, rivers, elephants — and two capitals, each chosen for its moment.

33
00:03:42,433 --> 00:03:43,250
Dig Quiz.

34
00:03:43,250 --> 00:03:44,803
Pause and name it.

35
00:03:44,803 --> 00:03:49,869
First: Magadha's first capital, protected by a ring of hills.

36
00:03:49,869 --> 00:03:51,067
Rajagriha.

37
00:03:54,833 --> 00:04:00,436
Second: the dynasty remembered for an enormous army and a famously large treasury.

38
00:04:00,436 --> 00:04:01,246
The Nandas.

39
00:04:01,246 --> 00:04:05,664
Third: the confederacy that Ajatashatru took years to break.

40
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:10,018
The Vajjis.

41
00:04:10,018 --> 00:04:15,975
In Mains, a standard question asks why Magadha rose above the other mahajanapadas.

42
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:24,943
Lead with geography — rivers, iron, elephants, fertile soil — then add the ambition of its rulers,

43
00:04:24,943 --> 00:04:28,252
and note that the sources are partisan about them.

44
00:04:32,500 --> 00:04:39,777
A section of city wall takes its place in the gallery: the most expensive thing a state could build, and the surest sign of one.

45
00:04:44,667 --> 00:04:51,048
Next gallery: a prince from a small republic walks out of his house, and changes the subcontinent.

46
00:04:51,048 --> 00:04:55,022
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

